# JewelFlow STAGING — subscription payment reconciliation (staging only).
#
# /etc/cron.d format: five timing fields, THEN the user field (www-data), then
# the command. This is NOT a personal `crontab -u` file (which omits the user),
# so the www-data field is mandatory — a system-crontab line without it is a
# syntax error and silently never runs.
#
# This is a DEDICATED cron for ONE command — NOT `artisan schedule:run`. It must
# never drive the full Laravel scheduler on staging (that would also fire fraud/
# shop-health/evaluate-alerts and every other scheduled job).
#
# flock (-n = non-blocking, exit immediately if a prior run still holds the lock)
# gives the same overlap protection as the in-app withoutOverlapping(20) guard on
# the scheduled definition, so a slow run never stacks a second reconcile.
# Lock file lives under STAGING storage — never /etc, never a shared path.
#
# Routine stdout/stderr is discarded (>/dev/null 2>&1): genuine failures surface
# through Laravel logging (storage/logs), so this cron cannot grow an unbounded
# raw log. If per-run output is ever wanted, drop the redirect and add a
# logrotate stanza for the target file instead.
#
# Every 10 minutes, bounded: 25 payments/run, 2 days back, 200ms between
# provider-touching reconciles (Razorpay rate-limit safety).
#
# Install to /etc/cron.d/ on STAGING ONLY, root-owned, mode 0644; verify syntax
# before activation. Do NOT add to the production scheduler. Reviewed artifact.
*/10 * * * * www-data /usr/bin/flock -n /var/www/jewelflow-staging/storage/framework/reconcile-payments.lock /usr/bin/php /var/www/jewelflow-staging/artisan subscription:reconcile-payments --limit=25 --days=2 --sleep-ms=200 >/dev/null 2>&1
